          "message": {
            "text": "Zero-length certificate BIT STRING underflows into an unbounded memcpy\n\nIn the default build (TLS_FULL_X509 is not defined), the ASN.1 certificate parser passes the outer signatureValue BIT STRING to tls_certificate_set_sign_key(). For a zero-length BIT STRING (DER 03 00), length is 0; tls_certificate_set_sign_key reads val[0], the byte after the TLV, and when that byte is zero it decrements len to -1. tls_certificate_set_copy then treats -1 as a non-zero length, calls TLS_MALLOC(len + 1) == malloc(0), and on glibc receives a non-NULL pointer, after which memcpy(*member, val, (size_t)-1) and (*member)[-1] = 0 corrupt memory far beyond the allocation. The certificate is parsed before the application certificate_verify callback, so an untrusted TLS peer reaches this pre-verification.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Memory corruption\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-191, CWE-787, CWE-125\n\nRemediation:\nRequire length >= 1 before reading val[0] and reject zero-length signature BIT STRINGs. In tls_certificate_set_copy, reject negative lengths explicitly instead of relying on the truthiness of an int. Do not allocate zero bytes for a non-empty logical value.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Feed a certificate whose outer signatureValue is 03 00 followed by 00 and assert TLS_BROKEN_PACKET or TLS_UNSUPPORTED_CERTIFICATE without a memcpy.\n- Run the certificate parser under ASan and assert no heap-buffer-overflow for zero-length BIT STRING inputs."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
