          "message": {
            "text": "Overlapping DTLS fragments overflow the stack reassembly buffer\n\ntls_consume_stream() accumulates each DTLS handshake fragment's fragment_length into fragment->written without ever checking that written + fragment_length still fits the declared data_length. The backing heap buffer is allocated as data_length * sizeof(char *), so repeated overlapping fragments (fragment_offset = 0) keep the heap copies in bounds while written grows far past data_length. A later DTLS record with data_length == fragment_length takes the non-fragmented path and reaches tls_parse_payload(), which copies fragment->written bytes into a 0x4000C-byte stack buffer. A remote sender can drive written to 0x4FB00 with five 0xFF00-byte fragments (each record fits in a UDP datagram) and overflow the stack by roughly 64 KB with attacker-controlled and uninitialized heap bytes.\n\nSeverity: high\n\nCategory: Memory corruption\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-121, CWE-787\n\nRemediation:\nTrack filled bytes independently of the declared length, reject any fragment for which fragment_offset + fragment_length exceeds data_length or for which written + fragment_length would exceed data_length, size the fragment allocation to data_length (not data_length * sizeof(char *)), and require written + 12 <= sizeof(local_buffer) before the stack copy. Prefer rejecting overlapping fragments entirely.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Send five overlapping 0xFF00-byte fragments with data_length=0x40000 followed by a small data_length==fragment_length record; assert TLS_BROKEN_PACKET before the memcpy and no ASan report.\n- Fuzz DTLS fragment_offset/fragment_length/data_length combinations and assert written never exceeds data_length."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:804a6edaaf2b335902b3e42397bc3b9582cd35c4739a7324b6033f627abe01c4"
          },
