                  "endLine": 13258,
                  "startLine": 13252
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "message": {
            "text": "STUN USERNAME and REALM attributes overflow the credential key buffer\n\ntls_stun_parse() accepts a USERNAME attribute of up to 513 bytes and a REALM attribute of up to 763 bytes, then concatenates username + ':' + realm + ':' + password into char key[0x4CE] (1230 bytes). Even before the 24-byte local password is appended, 513 + 1 + 763 = 1277 bytes overwrite the end of key by 47 bytes. The code also advances the pointer by username_len instead of realm_len after copying the realm. The overflow happens while processing MESSAGE-INTEGRITY, before the HMAC comparison, so a sender does not need valid credentials.\n\nSeverity: high\n\nCategory: Memory corruption\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-121, CWE-787\n\nRemediation:\nCompute the total credential length first and reject unless username_len + 1 + realm_len + 1 + pwd_len + 1 <= sizeof(key). Advance ptr by realm_len after the realm copy, cap both attributes to values that leave room for the password and separators, and avoid placing attacker-sized concatenations in a fixed stack buffer.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Send a binding request with a 513-byte USERNAME, a 763-byte REALM, and a 20-byte MESSAGE-INTEGRITY value; assert the parser rejects the credential build without writing past key.\n- Repeat with a valid MESSAGE-INTEGRITY and assert the same bound holds."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:e67b1488406f2c2f71f0dd51fce60f7fb2fa0f0bf3ef16195b34bd5669805c53"
          },
